# Permanent redirects for renamed URLs.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Until August 2026 the site had no redirect mechanism at all:
# no `redirects` key in astro.config.mjs, no _redirects, and worker.js passes everything
# outside /api/* straight to env.ASSETS.fetch with not_found_handling = "404-page". That
# meant renaming any live slug silently 404'd a URL Google had already indexed, which on
# a site whose growth depends on ranking is real harm rather than untidiness.
#
# THE STANDING RULE, from MAIN, 20 August 2026: any slug rename anywhere on this site
# ships its 301 in the SAME commit as the rename. This file is not a one-off for the
# entry-levies wave. It is where that rule lives from now on.
#
# HOW IT WORKS. Cloudflare Workers with static assets parse a plain-text `_redirects` in
# the asset directory. Astro copies everything in public/ verbatim into dist/, which is
# the directory wrangler.toml points [assets] at, so this file lands where Workers reads
# it and is never served as a page itself.
#
# THREE THINGS THAT ARE EASY TO GET WRONG:
#   1. THE CODE IS NOT OPTIONAL. Cloudflare defaults an omitted code to 302 (temporary),
#      which passes no ranking to the new URL. Every line here states 301 explicitly. A
#      302 would look like it worked and quietly discard the thing the redirect exists
#      to protect.
#   2. BOTH SLASH FORMS. Astro builds each page to {path}/index.html, so a reader or a
#      backlink can arrive at either /old or /old/. Each rename therefore gets two lines.
#   3. ORDER MATTERS. The first matching line wins, and static rules must precede any
#      wildcard. There are no wildcards here yet; keep it that way if you can, because a
#      wildcard over a country slug would capture spokes nobody intended.
#
# Redirects are NOT applied to requests worker.js serves itself. That is harmless here:
# worker.js owns only /api/notify and /api/rates, and run_worker_first is scoped to
# ["/api/*"], so every page request reaches the static-asset path where these apply.
#
# Format: [source] [destination] [code]

# ---- entry-levies wave, 20 August 2026 ----

# Brazil's entry spoke was the odd one out: it is an e-visa, the other three e-visa
# countries were already slugged e-visa, and "Brazil e-visa" is the search term.
/brazil/entry-visa   /brazil/e-visa   301
/brazil/entry-visa/  /brazil/e-visa   301

# The only slug on the site that was a lie about its own content. /mexico/entry-fees is
# about Chichen Itza tickets and cenote admission, not border costs, so it promised entry
# fees to anyone searching for them and delivered ruin admission. It also collided head-on
# with the entry-cost family. Its h1 already said "What do attractions really cost in
# Mexico?", so the slug was the part that was wrong.
/mexico/entry-fees   /mexico/attraction-fees   301
/mexico/entry-fees/  /mexico/attraction-fees   301

# Refund-spoke slug standardization, Aug 2026. The family moved to tax-refund
# because Japan and others charge a consumption tax or GST, not VAT.
/france/vat-refund               /france/tax-refund            301
/france/vat-refund/              /france/tax-refund            301
/italy/vat-refund                /italy/tax-refund             301
/italy/vat-refund/               /italy/tax-refund             301
/spain/vat-refund                /spain/tax-refund             301
/spain/vat-refund/               /spain/tax-refund             301
/united-kingdom/vat-refund       /united-kingdom/tax-refund   301
/united-kingdom/vat-refund/      /united-kingdom/tax-refund   301
/saudi-arabia/vat-refund         /saudi-arabia/tax-refund     301
/saudi-arabia/vat-refund/        /saudi-arabia/tax-refund     301
/portugal/iva-tax-refund-kiosk   /portugal/tax-refund         301
